export const SEED_EVENTS: HistoricalEvent[] = [
  { id: 'great-pyramid', title: 'Great Pyramid of Giza', description: 'In the scorching heat of the Giza plateau, around 2560 BCE, tens of thousands of workers hauled 2.3 million limestone blocks — each weighing an average of 2.5 tons — to construct the most ambitious building project the ancient world had ever seen. Commissioned by Pharaoh Khufu of the Fourth Dynasty, the Great Pyramid was not merely a tomb but a cosmic machine: its four sides align almost perfectly with the cardinal directions, deviating by less than one-fifteenth of a degree, a feat of precision that still baffles engineers today.\n\nContrary to popular myth, the builders were not slaves. Archaeological evidence from nearby workers\' villages reveals they were skilled laborers organized into competing teams with names like "Friends of Khufu" and "Drunkards of Menkaure." They ate well — beef, bread, and beer — and received medical care for broken bones. The logistics alone were staggering: granite beams weighing up to 80 tons were quarried in Aswan, 800 kilometers to the south, and transported by river barge during the annual Nile flood.\n\nWhen completed, the Great Pyramid stood 146.5 meters tall, its faces sheathed in brilliant white Tura limestone that gleamed so brightly in the Egyptian sun it could be seen from miles away. For over 3,800 years, it held the record as the tallest human-made structure on Earth. Today, it is the last surviving Wonder of the Ancient World, and the only one you can still walk up to and touch — a monument so enduring that it has witnessed every major civilization that followed its creation.', year: -2560, endYear: -2540, eraId: 'ancient', latitude: 29.9792, longitude: 31.1342, category: 'construction', locationName: 'Giza, Egypt', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e3/Kheops-Pyramid.jpg', impactText: 'The mortar used in the Great Pyramid has been analyzed by modern chemists, but no one has been able to reproduce its exact formula — it is stronger than the stone blocks it holds together and has lasted over 4,500 years.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/fJBlEPOj4Fk', modelUrl: 'https://sketchfab.com/models/4a251113722f4d969b6cf2ca5f35c502/embed?autostart=1&ui_theme=dark' },
  { id: 'code-hammurabi', title: 'Code of Hammurabi', description: 'Around 1754 BCE, King Hammurabi of Babylon stood before a towering black diorite stele — over two meters tall and weighing four tons — and declared that the gods themselves had chosen him to bring justice to the land. Carved into this imposing stone were 282 laws that would govern every aspect of Babylonian life, from the wages of an ox-driver to the punishment for a shoddy builder whose house collapsed and killed its owner. It was not the first legal code in history, but it was by far the most comprehensive, and it announced a revolutionary idea: that the law should be written down where everyone could see it, so that "the strong should not harm the weak."\n\nThe code reveals a society far more complex than most people imagine. It regulated medical malpractice (a surgeon who blinded a patient would have his own hand cut off), set minimum wages, established consumer protection for buyers of defective goods, and even addressed the rights of women to own property and initiate divorce. Punishments were calibrated by social class — harm done to a nobleman carried stiffer penalties than the same act against a commoner — but the principle of proportional justice was groundbreaking: the punishment should fit the crime, not the whim of whoever held power.\n\nThe stele was eventually carried off as war plunder by the Elamites around 1150 BCE and taken to Susa in modern-day Iran, where French archaeologists rediscovered it in 1901. Today it stands in the Louvre in Paris, one of the most important legal documents ever created. Its influence echoes through millennia — the concept of written law, publicly displayed and applied consistently, is the bedrock on which every modern legal system rests.', year: -1754, eraId: 'ancient', latitude: 32.5421, longitude: 44.4209, category: 'political', locationName: 'Babylon, Iraq', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/64/P1050763_Louvre_code_Hammurabi_face_rwk.JPG', impactText: 'The Code of Hammurabi includes one of history\'s first "lemon laws" — if you sold someone a defective product, you were legally required to replace it or refund the purchase price, nearly 4,000 years before modern consumer protection.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/oDALXORbtR4', modelUrl: 'https://sketchfab.com/models/44584936406a4879b8e054420dac7e07/embed?autostart=1&ui_theme=dark' },
  { id: 'trojan-war', title: 'Trojan War', description: 'For three thousand years, the Trojan War lived only in poetry — a tale of gods, heroes, and a face that launched a thousand ships. Homer\'s Iliad, composed around the 8th century BCE but describing events centuries earlier, told of a ten-year siege waged by a coalition of Greek kings against the mighty walled city of Troy, all because the Trojan prince Paris had stolen Helen, the most beautiful woman in the world, from her husband King Menelaus of Sparta. It was a story so vivid that the ancient Greeks never doubted it was real.\n\nThen, in the 1870s, a self-taught German archaeologist named Heinrich Schliemann did the unthinkable: he took Homer literally and started digging. At Hisarlik in northwestern Turkey, he unearthed the ruins of not one but nine cities built on top of each other, spanning millennia. Layer VIIa, dating to around 1180 BCE, showed clear signs of violent destruction — charred walls, scattered arrowheads, and hastily abandoned streets. The archaeological world was electrified. Troy was real. Whether Helen was real, whether there was truly a hollow wooden horse, remains unknown — but a great and wealthy city at the crossroads of East and West had indeed been besieged and burned.\n\nThe legacy of the Trojan War extends far beyond archaeology. It gave Western literature its founding masterpiece, shaped Greek identity for centuries, and introduced archetypes — the cunning Odysseus, the wrathful Achilles, the doomed Hector — that still resonate in every war story told today. The very idea of a "Trojan horse" remains our go-to metaphor for hidden threats. Real or mythologized, the war at Troy is the story where history and storytelling first became inseparable.', year: -1200, eraId: 'ancient', latitude: 39.9575, longitude: 26.2389, category: 'war', locationName: 'Troy, Turkey', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/18/Giovanni_Domenico_Tiepolo_-_The_Procession_of_the_Trojan_Horse_in_Troy_-_WGA22382.jpg', impactText: 'When Heinrich Schliemann excavated Troy in the 1870s, he found a cache of gold jewelry he called "Priam\'s Treasure." He smuggled it out of the Ottoman Empire in his wife\'s shawl — it vanished during WWII and was secretly kept in Moscow until Russia admitted to having it in 1993.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/gQbZX9JEQsQ', modelUrl: 'https://sketchfab.com/models/c66791cb3fc047e7b8a6d521cd1de85c/embed?autostart=1&ui_theme=dark' },
  { id: 'founding-rome', title: 'Founding of Rome', description: 'The story Rome told about itself was soaked in blood from the very beginning. According to legend, twin brothers Romulus and Remus — sons of the war god Mars, suckled by a she-wolf on the banks of the Tiber — quarreled over where to build their new city. When Remus mocked his brother by jumping over the low walls Romulus had begun to raise on the Palatine Hill, Romulus killed him, declaring: "So perish anyone who shall hereafter leap over my walls." On April 21, 753 BCE, Rome was founded in fratricide — and it would grow into the most powerful city the ancient world had ever known.\n\nArchaeology partially supports the legend\'s timeline. Excavations on the Palatine Hill have uncovered hut foundations and pottery dating to the mid-8th century BCE, suggesting that a small settlement of Latin shepherds and farmers did indeed cluster on Rome\'s famous seven hills around this time. The early Romans were an unremarkable people surrounded by far more sophisticated neighbors — the Etruscans to the north, the Greek colonies to the south. Yet within five centuries, this village of thatched huts would conquer the entire Italian peninsula, and within eight, it would rule an empire stretching from Britain to Mesopotamia.\n\nWhat made Rome extraordinary was not its origin but its ambition. The Romans absorbed every culture they conquered — Etruscan engineering, Greek philosophy, Egyptian religion — and forged it all into something new. Their roads, laws, language, and architecture would outlast the empire itself. When you drive on a paved road, watch a trial by jury, or read a language descended from Latin, you are touching something that traces back to a cluster of mud huts on a hill beside the Tiber, where a legendary founder chose violence and vision in equal measure.', year: -753, eraId: 'ancient', latitude: 41.9028, longitude: 12.4964, category: 'political', locationName: 'Rome, Italy', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/She-wolf_suckles_Romulus_and_Remus.jpg/1024px-She-wolf_suckles_Romulus_and_Remus.jpg', impactText: 'The traditional date of Rome\'s founding — April 21, 753 BCE — is still celebrated today as "Natale di Roma" (Rome\'s Birthday), with costumed parades of gladiators and legionaries marching through the ancient city center every year.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/8NGBG5pKAAY' },
  { id: 'democracy-athens', title: 'Birth of Democracy', description: 'In 508 BCE, Athens was teetering on the edge of tyranny. A power struggle among aristocratic families threatened to plunge the city into civil war. Into this chaos stepped Cleisthenes, an unlikely reformer from one of Athens\' most powerful families, who proposed something radical: instead of letting the nobility fight over who would rule, why not let the people decide? He reorganized Athenian society from the ground up, breaking the power of the old tribal aristocracies by creating ten new "tribes" that mixed citizens from different regions, ensuring no single faction could dominate.\n\nThe system Cleisthenes built was astonishingly direct. There was no representative government, no parliament — every male citizen over 18 could speak and vote in the Assembly (Ekklesia), which met on a hillside called the Pnyx with a quorum of 6,000. Citizens voted on everything from tax policy to declarations of war by raising their hands. Juries numbered in the hundreds to prevent bribery. Officials were chosen by lottery rather than election, on the theory that elections favored the rich and well-connected while random selection gave every citizen an equal chance to serve. It was messy, noisy, and revolutionary.\n\nAthenian democracy was far from perfect by modern standards — women, slaves, and foreigners were excluded, meaning only about 10-20% of the population could participate. But the core idea was explosive: that ordinary people, not kings or priests, had the wisdom and the right to govern themselves. This experiment lasted nearly two centuries and produced an extraordinary cultural flowering — the philosophy of Socrates and Plato, the tragedies of Sophocles, the Parthenon itself. The word "democracy" — from demos (people) and kratos (power) — was born here, and it would take over two thousand years for the rest of the world to catch up.', year: -508, eraId: 'classical', latitude: 37.9838, longitude: 23.7275, category: 'political', locationName: 'Athens, Greece', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/da/The_Parthenon_in_Athens.jpg', impactText: 'Athenian democracy had a unique safety valve called "ostracism" — citizens could vote to banish any person they considered too powerful by scratching a name on a pottery shard (ostrakon). The "winner" was exiled from Athens for ten years.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/0fivQUlC7-8' },
  { id: 'roman-forum', title: 'Roman Forum Inauguration', description: 'Before it became the nerve center of an empire, the Roman Forum was a swamp. The marshy valley between the Palatine and Capitoline hills was transformed around 500 BCE when the Romans — possibly under the Etruscan kings — built the Cloaca Maxima, one of the world\'s earliest sewage systems, to drain the waterlogged ground. What emerged was a public square that would serve as the beating heart of Roman civilization for nearly a thousand years: a place where senators debated the fate of nations, generals paraded their conquests, merchants hawked their goods, and ordinary citizens gathered to hear the news of the day.\n\nAt its peak, the Forum was a dazzling architectural showcase. The Temple of Saturn housed the state treasury. The Rostra — a speaker\'s platform decorated with the bronze prows of captured warships — was where Cicero delivered his thundering orations and where Mark Antony held up Caesar\'s bloodied toga to whip a crowd into fury. The Via Sacra, Rome\'s oldest street, cut through its center, serving as the route for triumphal processions in which victorious generals rode chariots past cheering crowds, their faces painted red in imitation of Jupiter himself. Every major event in Roman public life — elections, trials, funerals of great men, religious festivals — happened here.\n\nAfter the fall of Rome, the Forum was gradually buried under centuries of debris, its marble temples quarried for building material, its grand spaces converted into cattle pastures. By the Middle Ages, Romans called it the "Campo Vaccino" — the cow field. It was not until the 18th and 19th centuries that systematic excavation began to reveal the ruins we see today, a haunting skeleton of columns and arches that still conveys the grandeur of the civilization that built it.', year: -500, eraId: 'classical', latitude: 41.8925, longitude: 12.4853, category: 'cultural', locationName: 'Rome, Italy', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/44/Forum_Romanum_panorama_2.jpg', impactText: 'The Forum\'s drainage system, the Cloaca Maxima, was so well engineered that parts of it are still functioning today — over 2,500 years after construction — carrying storm water through Rome and emptying into the Tiber River.' },
  { id: 'alexander-empire', title: 'Alexander the Great\'s Empire', description: 'When Alexander III of Macedon inherited his father\'s throne in 336 BCE at the age of twenty, he was already a battle-tested warrior who had been tutored by Aristotle himself. Within just thirteen years, he would conquer the largest empire the world had ever seen — a domain stretching from Greece to the borders of India, encompassing Egypt, Persia, Mesopotamia, and Central Asia. He never lost a single battle. His military genius was matched by breathtaking personal courage; at the siege of Multan in India, he leaped alone from a scaling ladder into the enemy fortress, fighting surrounded until his men smashed through the walls to reach him. He nearly died from an arrow that punctured his lung.\n\nAlexander did not merely conquer — he transformed. He founded over twenty cities bearing his name, the most famous being Alexandria in Egypt, which became the intellectual capital of the ancient world and home to the legendary Library. He encouraged his soldiers to marry local women and adopted Persian court customs, envisioning a fusion of Greek and Eastern cultures that historians call the Hellenistic world. Greek became the lingua franca from the Mediterranean to Afghanistan. Greek art merged with Buddhist traditions in Gandhara, producing some of the first images of the Buddha. The cultural ripple effects of his conquests lasted centuries.\n\nAlexander died in Babylon on June 10, 323 BCE, at the age of 32 — possibly from typhoid fever complicated by alcoholism, though poisoning theories persist to this day. When asked on his deathbed to whom he left his empire, he reportedly whispered, "To the strongest." His generals promptly tore the empire apart in a series of wars that carved the ancient world into rival kingdoms. But the Hellenistic civilization he sparked would endure until Rome swallowed it whole, and the Greek cultural influence he spread would form one of the foundation stones of Western civilization.', year: -323, eraId: 'classical', latitude: 40.6401, longitude: 22.9444, category: 'political', locationName: 'Thessaloniki, Greece', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cc/Alexander_%28Battle_of_Issus%29_Mosaic.jpg', impactText: 'Alexander\'s body was preserved in a gold coffin filled with honey and displayed in Alexandria for centuries. Roman emperors including Augustus and Caligula made pilgrimages to visit it. The tomb was eventually lost — and despite countless searches, no one has ever found it.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/bVjKL1zd-vQ', modelUrl: 'https://sketchfab.com/models/7dcb19d12bdf44e6bcd7d8787923e9a6/embed?autostart=1&ui_theme=dark' },
  { id: 'great-wall-begin', title: 'Great Wall Construction Begins', description: 'In 221 BCE, Emperor Qin Shi Huang had just accomplished what no one had managed in five centuries of constant warfare: he unified the seven warring states of China into a single empire. But his northern frontier was terrifyingly vulnerable to mounted nomadic raiders — the Xiongnu, whose horseback cavalry could strike deep into Chinese territory and vanish before any army could respond. Qin\'s solution was as ambitious as it was brutal: connect and extend the scattered defensive walls that earlier states had built into one continuous fortification across the entire northern border. Hundreds of thousands of conscripted laborers, soldiers, and convicts were marched to the frontier to begin the work.\n\nThe human cost was staggering. Workers toiled in extreme conditions — scorching deserts, frozen mountain passes, and remote grasslands far from home. Food was scarce, conditions were harsh, and the dead were sometimes buried within the wall itself. Chinese folklore calls it "the longest cemetery on Earth." Yet the engineering was remarkable: the wall followed the contours of ridgelines for maximum defensive advantage, incorporating watchtowers every few hundred meters that could relay smoke signals across hundreds of kilometers in hours — an ancient early-warning system that could mobilize armies faster than any messenger on horseback.\n\nWhat most people picture as "The Great Wall" — the imposing stone-and-brick fortification snaking across mountaintops — is mostly the Ming Dynasty reconstruction from the 14th-17th centuries CE. But the project Qin Shi Huang began in 221 BCE established the concept and the route. A comprehensive archaeological survey completed in 2012 measured the total length of all wall sections ever built at 21,196 kilometers — more than half the circumference of the Earth. It remains the largest construction project in human history, a monument to both extraordinary human achievement and extraordinary human suffering.', year: -221, eraId: 'classical', latitude: 40.4319, longitude: 116.5704, category: 'construction', locationName: 'Beijing, China', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fa/Great_Wall_of_China_July_2006.JPG', impactText: 'Despite the popular myth, the Great Wall of China is NOT visible from space with the naked eye — astronauts have confirmed it is too narrow. However, the wall\'s watchtower smoke-signal system could transmit a message across the entire frontier in just a few hours, faster than any horse.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/23oHqNEqRyo', modelUrl: 'https://sketchfab.com/models/eb94be2c56a14f009e6f0f2533c996d7/embed?autostart=1&ui_theme=dark' },
  { id: 'julius-caesar', title: 'Assassination of Julius Caesar', description: 'On the morning of March 15, 44 BCE — the Ides of March — Gaius Julius Caesar walked into a meeting of the Roman Senate at the Theatre of Pompey. He had been warned. His wife Calpurnia had begged him not to go, troubled by nightmares. A soothsayer had famously cautioned him to "beware the Ides of March." But Caesar, who had conquered Gaul, crossed the Rubicon, defeated Pompey in civil war, and been declared dictator perpetuo (dictator for life), was not a man given to fear. He waved away his concerns and entered the Senate without his bodyguards.\n\nWhat happened next was swift and savage. As Caesar took his seat, a senator named Tillius Cimber approached with a petition, grabbing Caesar\'s toga and pulling it from his shoulder — the signal. Casca struck the first blow from behind, a clumsy slash to the neck. Caesar spun around, grabbing Casca\'s arm and stabbing him with his stylus, shouting "Casca, you villain!" But then the others closed in — Brutus, Cassius, and at least twenty more conspirators, all drawing concealed daggers. Caesar fought back briefly but was overwhelmed. Ancient sources claim he received 23 stab wounds. He collapsed and died at the base of a statue of his old rival Pompey, his blood pooling on the marble floor of the Senate.\n\nThe conspirators, who called themselves the Liberatores, believed they were saving the Roman Republic from a tyrant. They were catastrophically wrong. Caesar\'s assassination did not restore the Republic — it destroyed it. The power vacuum triggered another round of devastating civil wars that would claim the lives of nearly all the conspirators and ultimately hand absolute power to Caesar\'s adopted heir, the 18-year-old Octavian, who would become Augustus, the first Roman Emperor. The Republic the assassins had killed to protect died with their victim.', year: -44, eraId: 'classical', latitude: 41.8955, longitude: 12.4823, category: 'political', locationName: 'Rome, Italy', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/Vincenzo_Camuccini_-_La_morte_di_Cesare.jpg', impactText: 'Caesar was so beloved by the common people that when his body was cremated in the Forum, the crowd was so frenzied with grief that they tore apart nearby buildings to feed the funeral pyre. A comet appeared in the sky shortly after, which Romans believed was Caesar\'s soul ascending to godhood.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/wgPymD-NBQU', modelUrl: 'https://sketchfab.com/models/16eea6a1895948cdb2f610e72a6d6e9c/embed?autostart=1&ui_theme=dark' },
  { id: 'colosseum', title: 'Colosseum Completed', description: 'When Emperor Vespasian began construction of the Flavian Amphitheatre in 72 CE, he chose the site with calculated political genius: the private pleasure lake of the despised Emperor Nero\'s Golden Palace. Where a tyrant had built himself a playground, Vespasian would give the Roman people the greatest entertainment venue the world had ever seen. Eight years and tens of thousands of workers later, his son Titus inaugurated the completed Colosseum in 80 CE with 100 consecutive days of games in which, according to the ancient historian Cassius Dio, over 9,000 wild animals were killed and thousands of gladiators fought.\n\nThe engineering was centuries ahead of its time. The elliptical structure stood 48 meters tall — roughly the height of a modern 15-story building — and could seat between 50,000 and 80,000 spectators. An ingenious system of numbered arches and staircases (vomitoria) could fill or empty the entire arena in minutes, a crowd-flow design that modern stadium architects still study. A massive retractable awning called the velarium, operated by a detachment of sailors from the Roman navy, shielded spectators from the sun. Beneath the arena floor lay the hypogeum — a labyrinth of tunnels, cages, and mechanical elevators that could raise wild animals, scenery, and combatants dramatically into the arena through trapdoors.\n\nThe spectacles held here were both magnificent and horrifying. Gladiatorial combat, wild beast hunts (venationes), public executions, and even re-enactments of famous battles drew crowds from across the empire. In the early years, the arena may have been flooded for staged naval battles (naumachiae). The Colosseum remained in active use for over four centuries, surviving earthquakes and fires. Today, roughly two-thirds of the original structure has been destroyed by earthquakes and stone-robbers, yet what remains is still the most iconic symbol of Imperial Rome — and the direct ancestor of every sports stadium on Earth.', year: 80, eraId: 'classical', latitude: 41.8902, longitude: 12.4922, category: 'construction', locationName: 'Rome, Italy', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/53/Colosseum_in_Rome%2C_Italy_-_April_2007.jpg', impactText: 'The Colosseum\'s hypogeum (underground network) contained 80 vertical shafts with elevator platforms powered by human-operated winches — allowing lions, bears, and even elephants to appear as if rising magically from the arena floor.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/TB5weRIYhjQ', modelUrl: 'https://sketchfab.com/models/535dc96e586f40bd956ea3cbff810055/embed?autostart=1&ui_theme=dark' },
  { id: 'fall-of-rome', title: 'Fall of the Western Roman Empire', description: 'On September 4, 476 CE, a teenage boy named Romulus Augustulus — whose very name was a cruel irony, combining Rome\'s legendary founder with its first emperor — was quietly deposed by the Germanic warlord Odoacer. There was no great battle, no dramatic last stand. The last Western Roman Emperor was simply told to step aside, given a pension, and sent to live in a villa in Campania. The empire that had ruled the Western world for five centuries ended not with a bang but with a bureaucratic shrug. Most people living at the time barely noticed.\n\nThe "fall" had been centuries in the making. By the 5th century, the Western Empire was a hollow shell. Decades of civil wars, economic collapse, plague, and the relentless pressure of Germanic migrations had gutted its military and administrative capacity. The once-mighty legions were now largely composed of Germanic mercenaries whose loyalty was to their own chiefs, not to Rome. The city of Rome itself had been sacked twice — by the Visigoths under Alaric in 410 CE and by the Vandals in 455 CE — events that sent shockwaves of horror across the Mediterranean world. Saint Jerome, writing from Bethlehem, wept: "The city which had taken the whole world was itself taken."\n\nHistorians have proposed over 200 different theories for why Rome fell — from lead poisoning to moral decay to climate change. The truth is likely a perfect storm of interconnected crises. But what matters most is what came after. The fall of Rome did not plunge Europe into immediate "darkness." Roman institutions, language, law, and Christianity persisted and transformed. The Eastern Roman Empire — Byzantium — would survive for nearly another thousand years. But the political unity of the Western Mediterranean was shattered, and the fragmented world that emerged — of competing kingdoms, feudal lords, and a powerful Church — would define the medieval era for the next millennium.', year: 476, eraId: 'classical', latitude: 41.9028, longitude: 12.4964, category: 'political', locationName: 'Rome, Italy', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/64/Cole_Thomas_The_Course_of_Empire_Destruction_1836.jpg', impactText: 'The last Western Roman Emperor\'s name, Romulus Augustulus ("little Augustus"), combined the names of Rome\'s legendary founder and its first emperor — as if history were writing its own poetic bookend to a civilization that lasted over a thousand years.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/3PszVWZNWVA' },
  { id: 'hagia-sophia', title: 'Hagia Sophia Completed', description: 'When Emperor Justinian I stepped through the doors of his completed cathedral on December 27, 537 CE, he allegedly whispered, "Solomon, I have surpassed thee" — comparing his achievement to the legendary Temple of Jerusalem. He was not exaggerating. The Hagia Sophia (Holy Wisdom) was unlike anything the world had ever seen: a vast interior space crowned by a dome that seemed to float unsupported in the air, 55 meters above the marble floor, pierced by a ring of forty windows that poured light into the nave like a golden halo. Visitors described the experience as stepping into heaven itself.\n\nThe engineering behind this miracle was revolutionary. Justinian\'s architects, Anthemius of Tralles and Isidorus of Miletus, were not traditional builders but mathematicians who essentially invented a new structural system. The massive central dome, 31 meters in diameter, is supported not by thick walls but by a series of pendentives — curved triangular surfaces that transfer the dome\'s weight to four massive piers. This solution to the problem of placing a round dome on a square base was so elegant that it influenced virtually every large domed building constructed afterward, from the Blue Mosque next door to the U.S. Capitol building in Washington. The interior was covered in over 30 million gold mosaic tiles, and the marble was quarried from across the empire — green from Thessaly, purple from Phrygia, white from the Marmara islands.\n\nFor 916 years, the Hagia Sophia served as the world\'s largest cathedral and the spiritual heart of Eastern Orthodox Christianity. When the Ottoman Sultan Mehmed II conquered Constantinople in 1453, he was so awestruck by the building that he immediately ordered it preserved and converted into a mosque, adding minarets and Islamic calligraphy to the interior while leaving the Christian mosaics largely intact behind plaster. Today, after serving as a museum from 1934 to 2020 and then reconverted to a mosque, it remains one of the most extraordinary buildings ever constructed — a living testament to two great civilizations.', year: 537, eraId: 'medieval', latitude: 41.0086, longitude: 28.9802, category: 'construction', locationName: 'Istanbul, Turkey', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/Hagia_Sophia_Mars_2013.jpg', impactText: 'The original dome of the Hagia Sophia partially collapsed in an earthquake just 21 years after completion. It was rebuilt 6 meters higher and slightly steeper — and that rebuilt dome has stood for over 1,400 years, surviving more than 50 significant earthquakes.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/KRPp3jzv1Tw', modelUrl: 'https://sketchfab.com/models/10ef66d9626b4cf79ddde09182ee786d/embed?autostart=1&ui_theme=dark' },
  { id: 'viking-expansion', title: 'Viking Expansion Begins', description: 'On June 8, 793 CE, a group of Norse warriors sailed out of the morning mist and fell upon the monastery of Lindisfarne, a tiny tidal island off the northeast coast of England. The monks — who had lived there in peaceful scholarship for over a century, producing masterpieces like the Lindisfarne Gospels — were utterly unprepared. The raiders killed some, enslaved others, and plundered the monastery\'s treasures of gold, silver, and jeweled reliquaries. The Anglo-Saxon scholar Alcuin, writing from the court of Charlemagne, was horrified: "Never before has such terror appeared in Britain. The church of St. Cuthbert is spattered with the blood of the priests of God."\n\nThe attack on Lindisfarne was not the first Viking raid, but it was the one that shattered the complacency of Christian Europe and announced the beginning of the Viking Age. Over the next three centuries, Norse seafarers would reshape the map of the world. They conquered and settled large swaths of England (the Danelaw), colonized Normandy in France (the Normans were literally "North-men"), established the Rus\' state that became Russia, traded along rivers deep into Central Asia, raided as far as Constantinople and North Africa, and settled Iceland, Greenland, and — around 1000 CE — became the first Europeans to reach North America, nearly five centuries before Columbus.\n\nWhat drove the Viking expansion remains debated: population pressure, political upheaval in Scandinavia, technological superiority in shipbuilding, or simply the lure of silver and glory. Their longships were engineering marvels — shallow-drafted enough to navigate rivers yet seaworthy enough to cross the open Atlantic, with a single square sail and a hull of overlapping oak planks so flexible it could ride ocean swells like a living creature. The Vikings were not merely raiders but also traders, explorers, poets, and state-builders whose three-century eruption permanently reshaped Europe, Russia, and the North Atlantic world.', year: 793, eraId: 'medieval', latitude: 55.6689, longitude: -1.7847, category: 'war', locationName: 'Lindisfarne, England', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a9/Oseberg_Ship_-_Viking_Ship_Museum_%28Oslo%29.jpg', impactText: 'Viking longships were so shallow-drafted (as little as 50 cm) that they could sail up rivers and even be carried overland between waterways. Using this technique, Swedish Vikings sailed from the Baltic Sea all the way to Constantinople via Russian rivers — a journey of over 2,500 kilometers.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/kge0c2mNmRQ', modelUrl: 'https://sketchfab.com/models/be732d0cefd14cf280ae3d9b6c61c5b9/embed?autostart=1&ui_theme=dark' },
  { id: 'genghis-khan', title: 'Mongol Empire Founded', description: 'In 1206, on the banks of the Onon River in the wind-scoured steppe of Mongolia, a gathering of tribal chiefs proclaimed a man named Temujin as "Genghis Khan" — Universal Ruler. He was around 44 years old, and his life until that point had been a brutal odyssey of survival: kidnapped as a child, enslaved as a teenager, betrayed by allies, and forced to claw his way to power through two decades of steppe warfare. But Temujin possessed a rare combination of military genius, political cunning, and organizational brilliance that would make him the most consequential conqueror in human history.\n\nGenghis Khan\'s military innovations were devastating. He organized his army on a decimal system — units of 10, 100, 1,000, and 10,000 — cutting across tribal loyalties to forge a unified fighting force of unprecedented discipline. Mongol cavalry could cover 100 kilometers in a single day, communicating via a sophisticated system of flags, torches, and mounted messengers. They employed psychological warfare on a massive scale, deliberately spreading terrifying rumors of their brutality to encourage cities to surrender without a fight. When cities resisted, the consequences were apocalyptic: the population of the Khwarezmian Empire (modern Iran and Central Asia) may have dropped by 90% during the Mongol invasion. Entire cities were wiped from the map.\n\nBy the time of Genghis Khan\'s death in 1227, the Mongol Empire stretched from the Pacific Ocean to the Caspian Sea. Under his successors, it would expand further — into Russia, the Middle East, China, and Korea — becoming the largest contiguous land empire in history, covering 24 million square kilometers. But Genghis Khan\'s legacy was not only destruction. The Mongol Empire created the first true free-trade zone across Eurasia, protected by the "Pax Mongolica." Silk Road trade flourished as never before. Ideas, technologies, and diseases flowed freely between East and West. Gunpowder, paper money, the compass, and printing — Chinese inventions — began their journey toward Europe, while the bubonic plague traveled the same routes in the opposite direction.', year: 1206, eraId: 'medieval', latitude: 47.9184, longitude: 106.9177, category: 'political', locationName: 'Karakorum, Mongolia', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/35/YuanEmperorAlbumGenghisPortrait.jpg', impactText: 'A 2003 genetic study estimated that roughly 16 million men alive today — about 0.5% of the world\'s male population — are direct descendants of Genghis Khan, making him arguably the most reproductively successful individual in human history.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/wUVvTqvjUaM', modelUrl: 'https://sketchfab.com/models/b783a334bf354634b413d59f41613ca5/embed?autostart=1&ui_theme=dark' },
  { id: 'black-death', title: 'The Black Death', description: 'In October 1347, twelve Genoese trading ships docked at the port of Messina in Sicily. The sailors aboard were dying — covered in mysterious black swellings the size of eggs that oozed blood and pus, burning with fever, and delirious with pain. The harbor authorities ordered the ships out immediately, but it was already too late. The rats aboard the ships — and the fleas they carried, infected with the bacterium Yersinia pestis — had already come ashore. Within five years, the Black Death would kill between 75 and 200 million people across Eurasia, annihilating roughly 30-60% of Europe\'s entire population. It was the deadliest pandemic in human history.\n\nThe plague traveled along trade routes with terrifying speed. From Sicily, it swept through Italy, reaching Florence by the spring of 1348. The writer Giovanni Boccaccio, who survived, described bodies piled in the streets, families abandoning their sick, and entire neighborhoods left empty. "How many valiant men, how many fair ladies, breakfasted with their kinfolk and that same night supped with their ancestors in the next world!" The plague crossed the Alps, ravaged France and Spain, reached England by June 1348, and pushed into Scandinavia and Russia by 1351. Entire villages were wiped out. In some regions, there were not enough living to bury the dead.\n\nParadoxically, the Black Death may have been the catalyst that ended the Middle Ages. With labor suddenly scarce, surviving peasants could demand higher wages and better conditions — when lords refused, it sparked massive uprisings like the English Peasants\' Revolt of 1381. Serfdom began to crumble. The Church, which had been unable to explain or stop the plague, lost credibility, opening space for new intellectual movements. Some historians argue that the Renaissance itself was partly born from the social upheaval of the plague — a civilization forced to rebuild reimagined everything, from art to economics to the very relationship between humanity and God.', year: 1347, eraId: 'medieval', latitude: 43.7696, longitude: 11.2558, category: 'natural', locationName: 'Florence, Italy', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/10/Thetriumphofdeath.jpg', impactText: 'The Black Death was so devastating that it took Europe over 200 years to recover its pre-plague population level. England\'s population in 1300 was about 5-6 million; it did not reach that number again until the late 16th century.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/ySClB6-OH-Q' },
  { id: 'gutenberg-press', title: 'Gutenberg Printing Press', description: 'Around 1440, in a cramped workshop in Mainz, Germany, a goldsmith and inventor named Johannes Gutenberg was perfecting a technology that would change the world more profoundly than any invention since the wheel. His breakthrough was not printing itself — the Chinese had been printing with carved wooden blocks for centuries — but the development of movable metal type: individual letter molds cast from a durable lead-tin-antimony alloy that could be arranged, printed, disassembled, and reused endlessly. Combined with an adapted wine press and a new oil-based ink that adhered to metal, Gutenberg\'s system could produce pages of text with unprecedented speed and consistency.\n\nGutenberg\'s masterpiece was his 42-line Bible, completed around 1455 — a work of such extraordinary beauty that it could pass for a hand-copied manuscript. Each of the approximately 180 copies took about three years to produce, but this was revolutionary: a single scribe would need the same time to copy just one Bible by hand. Tragically, Gutenberg himself never profited from his invention. His financial backer, Johann Fust, sued him for repayment of loans, won, and seized the printing equipment. Gutenberg died in relative obscurity in 1468. But his technology spread like wildfire: by 1500, an estimated 1,000 printing shops across Europe had produced over 20 million volumes.\n\nThe printing press did not just make books cheaper — it democratized knowledge itself and destabilized every institution built on controlling information. Martin Luther\'s 95 Theses, printed and distributed across Germany in weeks, ignited the Protestant Reformation; without the press, it might have remained a local academic dispute. Scientific discoveries could be shared rapidly and accurately. Literacy rates soared. The press made possible the Renaissance, the Scientific Revolution, and the Enlightenment. Five centuries later, when the internet arrived, many commentators reached for the same comparison: "This is the biggest change since Gutenberg."', year: 1440, eraId: 'medieval', latitude: 49.9929, longitude: 8.2473, category: 'discovery', locationName: 'Mainz, Germany', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/62/Gutenberg%27s_vintage_printing_press_in_the_Gutenberg_Museum_in_Mainz%2C_Germany_%2848988497077%29.jpg', impactText: 'Only 49 copies of Gutenberg\'s original Bible survive today, and they are among the most valuable books on Earth. A complete copy sold at auction in 1987 for $5.4 million, and a single leaf (one page) can fetch over $100,000.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/EuzAbE-kPkM', modelUrl: 'https://sketchfab.com/models/42c694acac9840679692ab06db4ce98e/embed?autostart=1&ui_theme=dark' },
  { id: 'columbus-americas', title: 'Columbus Reaches the Americas', description: 'At 2 a.m. on October 12, 1492, a sailor named Rodrigo de Triana aboard the Pinta spotted a white sand cliff gleaming in the moonlight and cried out "Tierra! Tierra!" After 36 days crossing the open Atlantic — a voyage most European scholars believed was suicide — Christopher Columbus and his crew of roughly 90 men had reached a small island in the Bahamas that the indigenous Taino people called Guanahani. Columbus, convinced he had reached the outer islands of Asia, named it San Salvador and claimed it for the Spanish Crown. He was wrong about where he was by an entire hemisphere, but the consequences of his error would reshape every continent on Earth.\n\nColumbus\'s voyage was not an act of lone genius. The Portuguese had been methodically exploring the African coast for decades, and the idea of sailing west to reach Asia was circulating among European navigators. What Columbus had — beyond considerable seamanship — was relentless salesmanship and extraordinary luck. He dramatically underestimated the circumference of the Earth (the ancient Greeks had actually calculated it more accurately), and had the Americas not existed, his crew would have starved in the open Pacific. He made four voyages to the Caribbean between 1492 and 1504, dying in 1506 still believing he had reached Asia, never knowing he had stumbled upon two vast continents unknown to Europeans.\n\nThe collision of the Old and New Worlds that Columbus initiated was the most consequential event of the last millennium. The "Columbian Exchange" sent potatoes, tomatoes, corn, and tobacco eastward, and horses, wheat, cattle, and smallpox westward. European diseases, against which indigenous populations had no immunity, devastated the Americas — some estimates suggest 90% of the pre-contact population of the Western Hemisphere died within a century. Entire civilizations — the Taino, the Aztec, the Inca — were destroyed or subjugated. The wealth extracted from the Americas funded European empires and reshaped the global economy, while the forced transport of millions of enslaved Africans created a transatlantic horror whose consequences endure to this day.', year: 1492, eraId: 'renaissance', latitude: 24.0667, longitude: -74.5167, category: 'discovery', locationName: 'San Salvador, Bahamas', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6a/John_Vanderlyn_-_Columbus_Landing_at_Guanahani%2C_1492_-_WGA24269.jpg', impactText: 'Columbus\'s first voyage was funded for about 2 million maravedis (roughly $15,000 in today\'s money). The return on investment was incalculable: within 50 years, Spain was extracting more gold and silver from the Americas than existed in all of Europe combined.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/GD3dgiDreGc' },
  { id: 'manhattan-purchase', title: 'Purchase of Manhattan', description: 'In 1626, Peter Minuit, the newly arrived Director of the Dutch colony of New Netherland, conducted what is often called the most lopsided real estate deal in history: he purchased the island of Manhattan from the Lenape people for 60 guilders\' worth of trade goods — beads, cloth, metal tools, and other items. A 19th-century historian calculated this at $24, creating one of the most enduring legends in American folklore. But the reality is far more complicated than the myth. The Lenape likely viewed the transaction not as a permanent sale of land — a concept foreign to most Native American cultures — but as a temporary agreement to share the use of the territory. They had no idea they were signing away an island forever.\n\nThe Dutch settlement of New Amsterdam that grew on Manhattan\'s southern tip was unlike any other colonial outpost. From its founding, it was a polyglot trading hub where dozens of languages were spoken and tolerance was a business necessity. Dutch merchants, Walloon refugees, English dissenters, Sephardic Jews fleeing the Portuguese Inquisition in Brazil, free and enslaved Africans, and Lenape traders all mingled on the muddy streets around what is now Wall Street (named for an actual wooden wall the Dutch built as a northern defensive barrier). The settlement\'s commercial DNA — trade above all, profit as the organizing principle — would define the city\'s character for the next four centuries.\n\nIn 1664, the English seized New Amsterdam without a fight and renamed it New York. The island that Peter Minuit had purchased for the equivalent of a few boxes of supplies would become the financial capital of the world. Today, the total real estate value of Manhattan alone exceeds $1.7 trillion. The 59-square-kilometer island is home to Wall Street, the United Nations, Times Square, and Central Park — a concentration of economic, cultural, and political power unmatched by any comparable patch of ground on Earth.', year: 1626, eraId: 'renaissance', latitude: 40.7128, longitude: -74.0060, category: 'political', locationName: 'Manhattan, New York', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e2/GezichtOpNieuwAmsterdam.jpg', impactText: 'If the 60 guilders Peter Minuit paid for Manhattan in 1626 had been invested at 6.5% compound interest, it would be worth over $2 trillion today — roughly equal to Manhattan\'s actual current real estate value, making it a surprisingly fair deal by the math.' },
  { id: 'french-revolution', title: 'French Revolution', description: 'On the morning of July 14, 1789, a Parisian mob — hungry, furious, and armed with muskets looted from the Hotel des Invalides — surged toward the Bastille, a medieval fortress-prison in the heart of Paris that had become the hated symbol of royal tyranny. The garrison of 82 invalides (retired soldiers) and 32 Swiss guards held out for hours, but by afternoon the outer courtyard was breached. The governor, the Marquis de Launay, surrendered and was dragged into the street, where the crowd stabbed him to death and paraded his head on a pike. Only seven prisoners were found inside, but that hardly mattered. The storming of the Bastille was the thunderclap that announced the death of the old order.\n\nThe Revolution had been building for decades. France was bankrupt — largely from funding the American Revolution, ironically — and its tax system was grotesquely unfair: the nobility and clergy paid almost nothing while the common people were crushed. A series of bad harvests had driven bread prices to record highs; by 1789, a laborer spent roughly 88% of his wages just on bread. When King Louis XVI summoned the Estates-General for the first time in 175 years to address the fiscal crisis, the representatives of the common people (the Third Estate) broke away to form the National Assembly, vowing not to disband until France had a constitution. The king\'s attempt to shut them down by force ignited the explosion.\n\nWhat followed was a decade of upheaval that remade not just France but the entire Western world. The Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen established that sovereignty belonged to the nation, not the king. The monarchy was abolished, Louis XVI and Marie Antoinette were guillotined, and the Revolution devoured its own children during the Terror, when Robespierre sent thousands to the blade before he himself was executed. Out of the chaos rose Napoleon Bonaparte, who would spread revolutionary ideals — and French armies — across all of Europe. The Revolution\'s principles of liberty, equality, and popular sovereignty became the template for virtually every democratic movement that followed, from the Haitian Revolution to the Arab Spring.', year: 1789, eraId: 'renaissance', latitude: 48.8532, longitude: 2.3692, category: 'political', locationName: 'Paris, France', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5d/Eug%C3%A8ne_Delacroix_-_Le_28_Juillet._La_Libert%C3%A9_guidant_le_peuple.jpg', impactText: 'During the Reign of Terror, the guillotine was so busy that residents near the Place de la Revolution complained about the smell of blood. An estimated 16,594 people were officially sentenced to death, but the true toll including extrajudicial killings may have exceeded 40,000.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/8qRZcXIODNU' },
  { id: 'steam-locomotive', title: 'First Steam Locomotive', description: 'On February 21, 1804, in the industrial town of Merthyr Tydfil in south Wales, a Cornish engineer named Richard Trevithick made a bet that would change the world. He wagered 500 guineas that his new steam-powered locomotive could haul 10 tons of iron along the Penydarren tramway, a 15-kilometer route connecting an ironworks to the Merthyr-Cardiff canal. The ungainly machine — belching steam, clanking and groaning — completed the journey in just over four hours, carrying not only the iron but also 70 curious men who had climbed aboard for the ride. It was the first time in history that a steam locomotive had successfully hauled a load on rails.\n\nTrevithick\'s locomotive was ahead of its time — literally. The cast-iron tramway plates, designed for horse-drawn wagons, kept cracking under the engine\'s 5-ton weight, and the Penydarren locomotive was eventually converted into a stationary engine. Trevithick himself, a brilliant but commercially hapless inventor, never profited from his breakthrough and died penniless in 1833. It would take another two decades for George Stephenson to solve the practical problems and launch the first public steam railway — the Stockton and Darlington Railway in 1825, followed by the epochal Liverpool and Manchester Railway in 1830.\n\nBut Trevithick\'s proof of concept in that Welsh valley was the spark that ignited the Railway Age. Within 50 years, over 30,000 kilometers of railroad track crisscrossed Britain, and the technology had spread to every continent. Railways didn\'t just move goods faster — they restructured human civilization. They created standard time zones (before railways, every town set its own clocks). They enabled the growth of suburbs, national markets, and mass tourism. They made possible the rapid mobilization of armies that defined modern warfare. The Industrial Revolution was powered by coal and steam, but it moved on rails — and it all began with a bet on a Welsh hillside.', year: 1804, eraId: 'industrial', latitude: 51.7507, longitude: -3.3791, category: 'discovery', locationName: 'Merthyr Tydfil, Wales', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3f/Stephenson%27s_Rocket_drawing.jpg', impactText: 'Before railways, every town in Britain kept its own local time based on the sun. A town just 15 kilometers west could be several minutes behind. Railways made this chaos dangerous, so in 1847 the Railway Clearing House imposed "Railway Time" — the origin of standardized time zones used worldwide today.' },
  { id: 'suez-canal', title: 'Suez Canal Opens', description: 'On November 17, 1869, a flotilla of ships led by the French imperial yacht L\'Aigle, carrying Empress Eugenie of France, sailed into a canal that should not have existed. The Suez Canal — 164 kilometers of waterway cut through the Egyptian desert connecting the Mediterranean Sea to the Red Sea — had been dismissed as an impossible fantasy by most engineers, including the British, who actively tried to sabotage the project. But Ferdinand de Lesseps, a French diplomat with no engineering background but limitless charisma and connections, had spent 15 years turning the dream into reality. The opening ceremony was the most extravagant party of the 19th century, with 6,000 guests, a new opera house in Cairo (Verdi\'s Aida was commissioned for the occasion, though it premiered late), and fireworks that lit up the desert sky.\n\nThe construction had been brutal. Work began in 1859 with corvee labor — essentially forced Egyptian peasants, up to 20,000 at a time, digging with picks and baskets in the scorching heat. Thousands died from cholera, dysentery, and exhaustion. When international pressure ended the corvee system in 1863, de Lesseps pivoted to steam-powered dredges and excavators that ultimately moved 74 million cubic meters of earth. The project cost over double its initial estimate and nearly bankrupted Egypt — the Khedive was eventually forced to sell his shares in the canal to the British government for 4 million pounds, a bargain that gave Britain de facto control of the waterway for nearly a century.\n\nThe canal\'s impact on global trade was immediate and revolutionary. The voyage from London to Bombay, previously a 19,800-kilometer journey around the Cape of Good Hope taking four to six months, was cut nearly in half. World shipping patterns were redrawn overnight. Control of the canal became a geopolitical obsession — Britain stationed troops there for decades, and the Suez Crisis of 1956, when Egypt nationalized the canal, became one of the defining moments of the Cold War. Today, roughly 12% of all global trade passes through this narrow strip of water, and a single ship blocking the canal in 2021 (the Ever Given incident) cost the global economy an estimated $9.6 billion per day.', year: 1869, eraId: 'industrial', latitude: 30.4574, longitude: 32.3499, category: 'construction', locationName: 'Suez, Egypt', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/36/Suez.jpg', impactText: 'The idea of a canal at Suez is ancient — Pharaoh Necho II began digging one around 600 BCE, and a version connecting the Nile to the Red Sea functioned intermittently for over a thousand years. Napoleon surveyed the route in 1798 but abandoned the idea after his engineers incorrectly calculated a 10-meter difference in sea levels between the Mediterranean and the Red Sea.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/i9p-a8YJO-o' },
  { id: 'eiffel-tower', title: 'Eiffel Tower Completed', description: 'When Gustave Eiffel proposed a 300-meter iron tower for the 1889 World\'s Fair — commemorating the centennial of the French Revolution — the Parisian cultural establishment erupted in outrage. A petition signed by 300 prominent artists and intellectuals, including Guy de Maupassant, Alexandre Dumas fils, and Charles Garnier (architect of the Paris Opera), denounced the project as "a gigantic black smokestack" and "a hateful column of bolted sheet metal" that would disfigure the Paris skyline. Maupassant himself reportedly ate lunch at the tower\'s restaurant every day — because it was the only place in Paris from which he couldn\'t see the tower.\n\nThe construction was a masterpiece of industrial engineering. Over two years, 300 workers assembled 18,038 pieces of puddled iron (a type of wrought iron), connected by 2.5 million rivets, using detailed blueprints that specified the position of every single rivet hole. Despite working at dizzying heights — the tower rose to 300 meters, nearly twice the height of any existing structure — not a single worker was killed during construction (one death occurred during the installation of the elevators after the structure was completed). The four gracefully curving legs, which critics had mocked as ugly, were actually calculated using sophisticated wind-resistance equations; the tower\'s shape is essentially a mathematical curve that minimizes wind stress, a principle now fundamental to structural engineering.\n\nThe Eiffel Tower was meant to be temporary — its permit was for 20 years, after which it would be dismantled. It was saved by its usefulness as a radio transmission antenna; in 1910, it transmitted the first radio time signal broadcast across the globe, and during World War I, it intercepted enemy wireless communications that helped turn the tide of the Battle of the Marne. Today, the tower that Paris once despised is its most beloved icon, attracting nearly 7 million visitors annually and serving as the most-visited paid monument in the world. It has been repainted 19 times in its history, requiring 60 tons of paint each time.', year: 1889, eraId: 'industrial', latitude: 48.8584, longitude: 2.2945, category: 'construction', locationName: 'Paris, France', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/95/Construction_of_the_Eiffel_Tower.JPG', impactText: 'The Eiffel Tower grows and shrinks with the seasons: thermal expansion of the iron causes it to be up to 15 centimeters taller in summer heat than in winter cold, and it leans slightly away from the sun as the heated side expands.', modelUrl: 'https://sketchfab.com/models/a68b9ffb898d4aa7a44505926a536ce3/embed?autostart=1&ui_theme=dark' },
  { id: 'ww1', title: 'World War I Begins', description: 'On June 28, 1914, a 19-year-old Bosnian Serb named Gavrilo Princip stepped forward on a Sarajevo street corner and fired two shots from a Browning pistol into the open car carrying Archduke Franz Ferdinand, heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne, and his wife Sophie. Both died within the hour. It was a local act of political violence in a provincial city — but it triggered a cascade of ultimatums, mobilizations, and alliance obligations that, within six weeks, had plunged all of Europe into the most devastating war the world had ever seen. By the time the guns fell silent on November 11, 1918, over 17 million people were dead, four empires had collapsed, and the world that existed before the war was gone forever.\n\nThe war that everyone expected to be "over by Christmas" instead became a four-year nightmare of industrial slaughter. On the Western Front, opposing armies dug into a continuous line of trenches stretching 700 kilometers from the Swiss border to the English Channel, and for years neither side could break through. The Battle of the Somme in 1916 cost over one million casualties for a territorial gain of barely 10 kilometers. New technologies — machine guns, poison gas, tanks, aircraft, and submarines — made killing efficient on an unprecedented scale while leaving the strategic stalemate unbroken. Soldiers lived in mud-filled trenches infested with rats, surrounded by the unburied dead, enduring constant shelling that produced a new psychological condition: shell shock.\n\nThe war\'s consequences were seismic. The Austro-Hungarian, Ottoman, Russian, and German empires all disintegrated. The map of Europe and the Middle East was redrawn, creating new nations — Czechoslovakia, Yugoslavia, Iraq, Syria — whose borders, drawn by diplomats with little regard for ethnic or cultural realities, would generate conflicts that persist to this day. The punitive Treaty of Versailles humiliated Germany and sowed the seeds of an even more terrible war just two decades later. The generation that survived the trenches was forever changed; their disillusionment fueled the modernist movements in art, literature, and philosophy that defined the 20th century.', year: 1914, eraId: 'modern', latitude: 48.2082, longitude: 16.3738, category: 'war', locationName: 'Vienna, Austria', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fa/Cheshire_Regiment_trench_Somme_1916.jpg', impactText: 'On Christmas Day 1914, soldiers along parts of the Western Front spontaneously declared an unofficial truce. German and British troops climbed out of their trenches, exchanged gifts, sang carols together, and even played football in no man\'s land — before returning to killing each other the next day.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/dHSQAEam2yc' },
  { id: 'ww2', title: 'World War II Begins', description: 'At 4:45 a.m. on September 1, 1939, without a declaration of war, the German battleship Schleswig-Holstein opened fire on the Polish military garrison at Westerplatte, and 1.5 million Wehrmacht soldiers poured across the Polish border in a coordinated assault by land and air. The Blitzkrieg — "lightning war" — combined fast-moving tanks, motorized infantry, and dive-bombing Stuka aircraft in a new form of warfare that shattered the Polish army in weeks. Warsaw, subjected to relentless aerial bombardment that killed tens of thousands of civilians, surrendered on September 27. Two days later, Britain and France, bound by treaty to defend Poland, declared war on Germany. The deadliest conflict in human history had begun.\n\nOver the next six years, the war engulfed every inhabited continent. Nazi Germany conquered most of Europe and launched the Holocaust — the systematic, industrialized murder of six million Jews and millions of others in death camps that represented humanity\'s darkest chapter. Japan\'s empire swept across East Asia and the Pacific. The Soviet Union, after losing 27 million people — more than any other nation — turned the tide at Stalingrad in the bloodiest battle ever fought. The war saw the development of radar, jet engines, ballistic missiles, and nuclear weapons. It ended with the atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki in August 1945, which killed over 200,000 people and ushered in the nuclear age.\n\nThe scale of World War II defies comprehension. An estimated 70-85 million people perished — roughly 3% of the world\'s entire population. Cities from London to Tokyo were reduced to rubble. The political map was redrawn: the European colonial empires began their collapse, the United States and Soviet Union emerged as rival superpowers, and the Cold War began. The United Nations was founded to prevent such a catastrophe from recurring. The Nuremberg Trials established that "following orders" was no defense for war crimes — a legal principle that shapes international law to this day. The world that emerged from the ashes of 1945 was fundamentally different from the one that had existed in 1939.', year: 1939, eraId: 'modern', latitude: 52.2297, longitude: 21.0122, category: 'war', locationName: 'Warsaw, Poland', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a5/Into_the_Jaws_of_Death_23-0455M_edit.jpg', impactText: 'The Soviet Union lost an estimated 27 million people in World War II — roughly 14% of its entire population. To put this in perspective, it means that for every single day of the war, an average of over 14,000 Soviet citizens died.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/_uk_6vfqwTA' },
  { id: 'moon-landing', title: 'Moon Landing', description: 'At 10:56 p.m. EDT on July 20, 1969, Neil Armstrong placed his left foot on the powdery gray surface of the Moon and spoke the most famous words of the 20th century: "That\'s one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind." An estimated 600 million people — one-fifth of the world\'s population — watched the grainy black-and-white television broadcast, holding their breath as a human being walked on another world for the first time in history. Nineteen minutes later, Buzz Aldrin joined him, and together they spent two and a half hours on the lunar surface, collecting 21.5 kilograms of moon rocks, planting an American flag, and conducting experiments — while Michael Collins orbited alone overhead in the command module, the loneliest human being in existence, cut off from all radio contact every time he passed behind the Moon.\n\nThe journey to this moment had been a decade-long sprint fueled by Cold War rivalry and national pride. In 1961, President John F. Kennedy had committed the United States to landing a man on the Moon before the end of the decade — at a time when America\'s total human spaceflight experience amounted to 15 minutes. NASA\'s budget swelled to nearly 4.5% of the federal budget (compared to less than 0.5% today), and at its peak the Apollo program employed over 400,000 people across 20,000 companies. The technical challenges were immense: three astronauts died in a launch pad fire during Apollo 1, and the program came within seconds of catastrophe multiple times.\n\nThe lunar module Eagle landed with less than 25 seconds of fuel remaining — Armstrong had been forced to take manual control and fly past the intended landing site when he saw it was strewn with boulders. The onboard guidance computer, which had less processing power than a modern pocket calculator and just 72 kilobytes of memory, threw multiple alarms during the descent that nearly aborted the landing. Between 1969 and 1972, twelve humans walked on the Moon across six Apollo missions. Then, abruptly, the program ended. No human has returned to the lunar surface since Apollo 17 in December 1972 — making the Moon landings, paradoxically, both humanity\'s greatest technological achievement and a capability we chose to abandon.', year: 1969, eraId: 'modern', latitude: 28.5721, longitude: -80.6480, category: 'discovery', locationName: 'Cape Canaveral, Florida', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/98/Aldrin_Apollo_11_original.jpg', impactText: 'The Apollo 11 astronauts had to go through U.S. Customs when they returned to Earth. Their customs declaration form listed their cargo as "moon rock and moon dust samples" and their departure point as "the Moon." They were also quarantined for 21 days in case they had brought back alien pathogens.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/QhULJr-LKbg', modelUrl: 'https://sketchfab.com/models/372bb6781922471cada4e0a9bd5c61fb/embed?autostart=1&ui_theme=dark' },
  { id: 'berlin-wall', title: 'Fall of the Berlin Wall', description: 'On the evening of November 9, 1989, an East German government spokesman named Gunter Schabowski held a press conference to announce new, slightly relaxed travel regulations. When a journalist asked when the new rules took effect, Schabowski shuffled through his papers, found no specific date, and shrugged: "Immediately, without delay." He had made a mistake — the regulations were supposed to take effect the next day, with orderly processing. But the news spread instantly. Within hours, tens of thousands of East Berliners surged toward the wall\'s checkpoints, demanding to cross. The overwhelmed border guards, receiving no clear orders from their superiors, opened the gates. The Berlin Wall — the most infamous symbol of the Cold War — had fallen, not through military force or political negotiation, but through a bureaucratic blunder at a press conference.\n\nThe Wall had divided Berlin for 28 years. Built almost overnight on August 13, 1961, by the East German government to stop the hemorrhage of citizens fleeing to the West, it was not one wall but two, separated by a "death strip" of raked sand (to show footprints), guard towers, anti-vehicle trenches, and tripwire-activated automatic guns. At least 140 people were killed trying to cross it, some shot in full view of West Berlin residents. The Wall became the physical manifestation of the Iron Curtain — the dividing line between communist East and capitalist West that had defined global politics since 1945.\n\nWhen the gates opened that November night, the scenes were extraordinary. Strangers embraced in tears. People danced on top of the Wall, something that would have gotten them shot 24 hours earlier. Champagne flowed freely. "Mauerspechte" (wall woodpeckers) arrived with hammers and chisels to chip off souvenir fragments. Within a year, Germany was officially reunified. Within two years, the Soviet Union itself had dissolved. The Cold War — which had threatened nuclear annihilation for four decades, fueled proxy wars across the globe, and divided families, cities, and entire nations — was over. An era that had defined the second half of the 20th century ended with a party, not a war.', year: 1989, eraId: 'modern', latitude: 52.5163, longitude: 13.3777, category: 'political', locationName: 'Berlin, Germany', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1c/West_and_East_Germans_at_the_Brandenburg_Gate_in_1989.jpg', impactText: 'The fall of the Berlin Wall happened by accident. Spokesman Gunter Schabowski was handed the new travel regulations minutes before the press conference without being properly briefed. His off-the-cuff answer — "immediately, without delay" — was never authorized, but once broadcast live on television, it could not be taken back.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/A9fQPzZ1-hg' },
  { id: 'www-invention', title: 'World Wide Web Invented', description: 'In March 1989, a 33-year-old British computer scientist named Tim Berners-Lee submitted a proposal to his bosses at CERN, the European particle physics laboratory in Geneva, Switzerland. The document, titled "Information Management: A Proposal," outlined a system for linking documents across different computers using hypertext — clickable connections that could jump from one piece of information to another regardless of where it was stored. His supervisor\'s handwritten note on the cover page read simply: "Vague but exciting." It was perhaps the greatest understatement in the history of technology. Berners-Lee was proposing the World Wide Web.\n\nOver the next two years, Berners-Lee built the foundational technologies that make the web possible: HTML (the language for creating web pages), HTTP (the protocol for transmitting them), and URLs (the addressing system for finding them). He wrote the first web browser and the first web server, both running on a NeXT computer in his office at CERN. The first website — info.cern.ch — went live on December 20, 1990. A handwritten note taped to the computer read: "This machine is a server. DO NOT POWER IT DOWN!!" The web was initially used only by physicists sharing research papers, and it grew slowly at first: by 1993, there were only about 600 websites in the world.\n\nThen came the inflection point. In 1993, CERN made the extraordinary decision to release the web\'s source code into the public domain, making it free for anyone to use and build upon. Berners-Lee himself insisted on this — he could have patented the technology and become one of the wealthiest people in history, but he believed the web\'s power depended on its universality. The effect was explosive. By 1995, there were 23,000 websites; by 2000, over 17 million. Today, there are nearly 2 billion websites, and over 5 billion people — more than 60% of humanity — use the internet. The web has transformed commerce, communication, politics, entertainment, science, and human relationships more profoundly than any invention since the printing press, and it all started with a "vague but exciting" memo at a physics lab.', year: 1989, eraId: 'modern', latitude: 46.2044, longitude: 6.1432, category: 'discovery', locationName: 'Geneva, Switzerland', imageUrl: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d1/First_Web_Server.jpg', impactText: 'The original NeXT computer that Tim Berners-Lee used as the first web server is now displayed at CERN with its original label still attached: "This machine is a server. DO NOT POWER IT DOWN!!" It is arguably the most important single computer in history.', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/J8hzJxb0rpc' },
];

